---
const guide = '/images/webmaker-guide';
---
<div class="tutorial-intro"><p>WebMAKER 0.1.1 is a public prerelease with an English guide, sample site and Windows Studio. The diagrams below explain the workflow; they are illustrations, not app screenshots.</p><p><a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/PlayMAKER-apps/webmaker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WebMAKER repository ↗</a> · <a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/PlayMAKER-apps/webmaker/releases/tag/v0.1.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Studio prerelease ↗</a></p></div>
<nav class="tutorial-paths" aria-label="Choose a tutorial path"><a href="#user-guide">For users: install & use</a><a href="#developer-guide">For contributors & developers</a></nav>
<div class="tutorial-path-heading" id="user-guide"><p class="eyebrow">For users</p><h3>Install & use WebMAKER</h3><p>Studio 0.1.1 is available for Windows. It opens a local club project, saves changes and guides publishing. Creating the first club repository still requires technical setup; the contributor section has those commands.</p></div>
<ol class="tutorial-steps">
  <li class="tutorial-step"><div class="step-copy"><span class="section-kicker">01 / Install</span><h3>Install Studio</h3><p><a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/PlayMAKER-apps/webmaker/releases/download/v0.1.1/WebMAKER.Studio_0.1.1_x64-setup.exe">Download WebMAKER Studio 0.1.1 for Windows ↗</a>. The installer is unsigned. Install Git for Windows with Git Credential Manager if you plan to publish from Studio. Studio does not store a personal access token.</p></div><figure><img src={`${guide}/02-studio.svg`} alt="Illustration of WebMAKER Studio club and theme editor, local save, validation and publishing" width="960" height="440" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Studio separates local save, push and completed deployment. Illustration.</figcaption></figure></li>
  <li class="tutorial-step"><div class="step-copy"><span class="section-kicker">02 / Club project</span><h3>Open your club project</h3><p>Open a local club folder in Studio. If you do not have one yet, ask a technical helper to create it from the WebMAKER starter. The starter offers Bold, Classic and Dynamic themes with synthetic data; it contains no Nerone club history or media. The setup commands are in the contributor section below.</p></div><figure><img src={`${guide}/01-themes.svg`} alt="Illustration comparing Bold, Classic and Dynamic layouts with the same Aurora club data" width="960" height="440" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One club, three site layouts. Illustration.</figcaption></figure></li>
  <li class="tutorial-step"><div class="step-copy"><span class="section-kicker">03 / Customize</span><h3>Add your identity and content</h3><p>Set your club name, logo, colors, sport and theme. Replace the sample teams, players, games and news. Keep IDs stable after publishing, and add only images you have permission to use.</p><p>For AI help, share synthetic examples and review every suggested change before publishing. Keep private player data and credentials out of prompts.</p></div></li>
  <li class="tutorial-step"><div class="step-copy"><span class="section-kicker">04 / Publish</span><h3>Preview and publish</h3><p>Save locally, validate and inspect Studio's publishing preview. When the club repository and GitHub Pages are configured, push the changes and wait for the Pages workflow to complete. Check the live site afterward. The first repository and Pages setup is covered in the contributor guide below.</p></div><figure><img src={`${guide}/03-pages.svg`} alt="Four illustrated steps: clone, customize, enable GitHub Pages Actions, verify the live site" width="960" height="440" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Publishing steps. Illustration.</figcaption></figure></li>
</ol>
<div class="tutorial-path-heading" id="developer-guide"><p class="eyebrow">For contributors & developers</p><h3>Improve the open source project</h3><p>WebMAKER is public under Apache-2.0 with a NOTICE attribution. Use the source, tests and shared format notes to propose focused changes.</p></div>
<div class="developer-guide-grid">
  <div class="developer-guide-card"><h4>1. Clone and initialize a club</h4><p>Install Node.js 22+ and Git. The initializer creates an independent club folder with synthetic data and a Pages workflow. In <code>club.yaml</code>, set the GitHub repository URL, Pages URL and base path before publishing. The <a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/PlayMAKER-apps/webmaker#user-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">repository guide ↗</a> has the full Git and Pages setup.</p><pre><code>git clone https://github.com/PlayMAKER-apps/webmaker.git
cd webmaker
node scripts/init-club.mjs ../my-club</code></pre></div>
  <div class="developer-guide-card"><h4>2. Build and test locally</h4><p>The site uses Node.js 22+; Studio also needs Rust/MSVC, Visual Studio Build Tools and WebView2 on Windows. Use synthetic fixtures. A successful push does not prove Pages deployment.</p><pre><code>cd ../my-club/site
npm ci
npm test
npm run build</code></pre></div>
  <div class="developer-guide-card"><h4>3. Propose a focused improvement</h4><p>Open an issue in <a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/PlayMAKER-apps/webmaker/issues">WebMAKER</a>, fork the repository, create a branch and send a pull request with a clear user workflow and tests. Good early contributions include an editor driven by sport profiles, accessibility checks, visual regression tests and extraction of the social graphics engine after asset review.</p></div>
  <div class="developer-guide-card"><h4>4. Respect shared data</h4><p>TagMAKER imports <code>teams.yaml</code>, <code>players/*.yaml</code> and <code>rosters.csv</code> from a WebMAKER club. Its 0.1 JSON event timeline is a separate contract from WebMAKER's per-game statistics CSV. Preserve stable IDs and provide migrations for schema changes. See the <a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/PlayMAKER-apps/playmaker-schema" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PlayMAKER schema repository ↗</a>.</p></div>
</div>
